.\" Manual page for ethdate.
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
.TH ETHDATE 1 "2025-09" "ethdate 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ethdate \- print and convert Ethiopian calendar dates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ethdate
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ DATE .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ethdate
prints the current date in the Ethiopian calendar, converts dates between the
Ethiopian, Gregorian and Julian calendars and the Julian Day Number, fixed day
number and Unix time scales, lists the holidays of an Ethiopian year, prints the
traditional
.I Baḥire Ḥasab
computus table, and draws a month grid in the manner of
.BR cal (1).
.PP
The Ethiopian calendar has thirteen months: twelve of thirty days and
.IR Ṗagumēn ,
a short thirteenth month of five days, or six in a leap year. A leap year is one
whose year number leaves a remainder of three when divided by four \(em three,
not zero, which is the single most common mistake in other implementations. The
year begins on 11 September in the Gregorian calendar, or 12 September in the
year preceding a Gregorian leap year. That correspondence holds only while the
Ethiopian and Gregorian leap cycles are in phase, which is to say until
1 March 2100; thereafter every skipped Gregorian century leap day moves it one
day later.
.PP
With no
.IR DATE ,
.B ethdate
prints today. A
.I DATE
may be:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
an ISO-shaped date,
.IR YYYY-MM-DD ,
read in the calendar named by
.BR \-\-from ;
.IP \(bu 2
one of
.BR now ,
.BR today ,
.B yesterday
or
.BR tomorrow ;
.IP \(bu 2
a Unix timestamp written as
.BR @1757548800 ;
.IP \(bu 2
a relative day count such as
.B +30
or
.BR \-7 ;
.IP \(bu 2
a bare number when
.B \-\-from
is
.BR jdn ,
.B rd
or
.BR unix ;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR \- ,
meaning read dates from standard input, one per line.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-date " \fIDATE\fR"
Convert
.IR DATE .
May be given more than once.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " \fIFORMAT\fR"
Format the output with the
.BR strftime (3)-style
grammar described under
.BR FORMAT .
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-from " \fICALENDAR\fR"
Read input dates in
.IR CALENDAR .
Default:
.BR ethiopic .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-to " \fICALENDAR\fR"
Write output dates in
.IR CALENDAR .
Default:
.BR ethiopic ,
except that
.B \-\-from
alone implies conversion to the Ethiopian calendar.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-gregorian ", " \-e ", " \-\-ethiopic ", " \-\-julian ", " \-\-jdn ", " \-\-rd ", " \-\-unix
Shorthands for
.BR \-\-to .
.TP
.BR \-I ", " \-\-iso
Print a bare
.I YYYY-MM-DD
with no names or era marker.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Print one JSON object per date, containing every calendar's view of it, the
weekday, day of year, leap-year flag, Amete Alem year and any holiday.
.TP
.B \-\-waybar
Print a single JSON object with the
.IR text ,
.IR alt ,
.I tooltip
and
.I class
keys that Waybar's
.I custom
module expects. The class is one of
.IR holiday ,
.IR observance ,
.I pagume
or
.IR ordinary ,
so holidays can be styled in CSS.
.TP
.BR \-\-polybar ", " \-\-i3blocks ", " \-\-i3status
Print one line suitable for a status bar, marking public holidays with a star.
.TP
.B \-\-locale \fILOCALE\fR
Use
.B am
for Amharic names or
.B en
for Latin ones. The default is taken from
.IR $LC_ALL ,
.I $LC_TIME
or
.IR $LANG ,
falling back to Amharic.
.TP
.B \-\-era \fIERA\fR
Number years in
.B am
.RI ( Amete
.IR Mihret ,
the default) or
.B aa
.RI ( Amete
.IR Alem ,
5500 years earlier).
.TP
.B \-\-day\-start \fIWHEN\fR
Choose when a calendar day turns:
.B dawn
(06:00 local, the traditional reckoning, the default) or
.BR midnight .
This matters between midnight and dawn, when the traditional and civil dates
differ.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-utc
Use UTC instead of the local zone.
.TP
.B \-\-offset \fI±HH:MM\fR
Use a fixed UTC offset instead of the local zone.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-time
Include the Ethiopian time of day. Ethiopian clock hours are counted from dawn,
so 1 o'clock in the morning is 07:00 by the Western clock.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-calendar " [\fIYEAR\fR]"
Print a month grid. With a
.I YEAR
the whole year is printed; with
.I YYYY-MM
that month is printed; with neither, the current month.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-year " [\fIYEAR\fR]"
Print all thirteen months of a year.
.TP
.B \-\-holidays " [\fIYEAR\fR]"
List the holidays of an Ethiopian year with their Ethiopian and Gregorian dates.
Islamic holidays observed in Ethiopia follow the Hijri calendar and are
deliberately not listed; see
.BR BUGS .
.TP
.B \-\-computus " [\fIYEAR\fR]"
Print the
.I Baḥire Ḥasab
table: the Amete Alem year, medeb, wenber, abektie, metqi, the evangelist of the
year and every movable feast.
.TP
.B \-\-is\-holiday
Exit 0 if the date is a public holiday and 1 if it is not, printing the name
unless
.B \-\-quiet
is given. Intended for shell conditionals.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-stdin
Read dates from standard input, one per record.
.TP
.BR \-0 ", " \-\-null
Separate input and output records with NUL rather than newline.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print no error messages; report failure through the exit status alone.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Never emit ANSI colour. Colour is also suppressed when standard output is not a
terminal or when
.I $NO_COLOR
is set.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH CALENDARS
.BR ethiopic ", " gregorian ", " julian ", " jdn ", " rd ", " unix .
.B rd
is the fixed day number of Reingold and Dershowitz, with R.D. 1 = 1 January 1
(Gregorian).
.SH FORMAT
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
%Y@year, zero padded to four digits
%y@year modulo 100
%C@century
%G@year in Ge'ez numerals, e.g. ፳፻፲፯
%m@month number, 1\(en13
%d@day of the month
%e@day of the month, space padded
%B@month name, per \fB\-\-locale\fR
%b@month name in Latin script
%h@three-letter month abbreviation
%A@weekday name, per \fB\-\-locale\fR
%a@three-letter English weekday
%q@weekday, Latin transliteration
%u@ISO weekday number, Monday = 1
%w@weekday number, Sunday = 0
%j@day of the year, 1\(en366
%E@era, Latin: E.C.
%N@era, Amharic: ዓ.ም.
%L@year in the Amete Alem era
%F@%Y\-%m\-%d
%D@%m/%d/%y
%g@the Gregorian equivalent, ISO form
%J@Julian Day Number
%R@fixed day number (R.D.)
%s@Unix timestamp of midnight UTC
%I@Ethiopian hour, 1\(en12
%K@Ethiopian hour, zero padded
%H@Western hour, 0\(en23
%M@minute
%S@second
%p@period of day, Amharic
%P@period of day, romanised
%T@%I:%M:%S %p
%Z@time-zone abbreviation
%z@UTC offset
%n %t %%@newline, tab, a literal %
.TE
.PP
A
.B \-
between the
.B %
and the specifier suppresses zero padding, as in
.BR %\-d .
An unknown specifier is reproduced verbatim, so a typo is visible rather than
silently dropped.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; or, with
.BR \-\-is\-holiday ,
the date is a public holiday.
.TP
.B 1
A date could not be parsed or converted; or, with
.BR \-\-is\-holiday ,
the date is an ordinary day.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong.
.PP
A broken pipe is not an error:
.B ethdate \-\-holidays | head \-1
exits 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.I TZ
The time zone, as a zoneinfo name, an absolute path to a TZif file, or a fixed
offset such as
.BR +03:00 .
When unset,
.I /etc/localtime
is read.
.TP
.IR LC_ALL ", " LC_TIME ", " LANG
Select the default script for names.
.TP
.I NO_COLOR
When set, colour is never emitted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Today, in Amharic:
.B ethdate
.TP
Today, with the Ethiopian time of day:
.B ethdate \-\-locale en \-T
.TP
Convert a Gregorian date:
.B ethdate \-F gregorian 2024\-09\-11
.TP
Convert back:
.B ethdate \-g 2017\-01\-01
.TP
The coming week, from a pipe:
.B seq 0 6 | sed 's/^/+/' | ethdate \-
.TP
Feed a script with JSON:
.B ethdate \-\-json | jq \-r .ethiopic.month_name
.TP
Act on today's status:
.B ethdate \-\-is\-holiday \-q || make \-C /srv/work
.TP
This year's holidays as a table:
.B ethdate \-\-holidays \-\-locale en
.TP
A Waybar module:
.B exec = ethdate \-\-waybar
.SH BUGS
Eid al\-Fitr, Eid al\-Adha and Mawlid are public holidays in Ethiopia but follow
the Hijri calendar, which this program does not implement; they are omitted
rather than approximated.
.PP
Genna is observed on 28
.I Taḫśaś
in years whose number is divisible by four, which keeps it on 7 January while
the leap cycles are in phase. Lalibela, and churches dedicated to
.IR "Bä'alä Ǝgziabḥer" ,
keep the canonical 29
.IR Taḫśaś ;
.B \-\-holidays
reports the widely observed date.
.PP
Report bugs at
.UR https://github.com/mitegab/pagumie/issues
